1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,304
ChatGPT doesn't actually know anything. It's guessing.

2
00:00:04,304 --> 00:00:10,222
One word at a time. And it's scary good at it.

3
00:00:10,222 --> 00:00:15,255
By the end of this video you'll understand what's actually happening when you hit enter.

4
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:18,365
And there's a question that'll bug you halfway through.

5
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:21,392
If it's only guessing, how can it solve problems? We'll get there.

6
00:00:22,697 --> 00:00:28,673
Let's play a game. Finish this sentence in your head.

7
00:00:28,673 --> 00:00:33,121
Peanut butter and... You said jelly. Or jam,

8
00:00:33,121 --> 00:00:35,893
depending on where you live. You didn't look it up.

9
00:00:35,893 --> 00:00:37,263
You've just seen those words together so many times that your brain filled the gap.

10
00:00:38,669 --> 00:00:45,045
Another one. The capital of France is... Paris. Obviously.

11
00:00:45,045 --> 00:00:47,351
That is basically the whole trick.

12
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:51,386
An AI like ChatGPT looks at everything written so far and asks one question.

13
00:00:51,386 --> 00:00:57,491
What comes next? Then it adds that piece, and asks again. And again.

14
00:00:57,491 --> 00:01:01,301
Hundreds of times, super fast, until you've got a full answer.

15
00:01:02,688 --> 00:01:05,089
If that sounds familiar, it should.

16
00:01:05,089 --> 00:01:09,462
Your phone does a tiny version of this. Try it after this video.

17
00:01:09,462 --> 00:01:12,513
Type "I'm going to" and keep tapping the middle suggestion.

18
00:01:12,513 --> 00:01:13,773
You'll get something like,

19
00:01:13,773 --> 00:01:17,858
"I'm going to be there in a few minutes and I will be there in a few minutes and..."

20
00:01:17,858 --> 00:01:20,729
It's nonsense. Your phone is guessing too, it's just really bad at it.

21
00:01:22,239 --> 00:01:27,032
So what's the difference between your phone and ChatGPT? Scale.

22
00:01:27,032 --> 00:01:29,394
Your phone learned from your texts.

23
00:01:29,394 --> 00:01:33,789
Models like ChatGPT learned from an enormous amount of text. Books,

24
00:01:33,789 --> 00:01:36,525
websites, articles, forums, code.

25
00:01:36,525 --> 00:01:39,767
More reading than any human could do in thousands of lifetimes.

26
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:42,719
Okay, the question that's been bugging you.

27
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:46,402
If it's only guessing the next word, how can it write working code,

28
00:01:46,402 --> 00:01:50,141
or explain a tax form, or help plan a wedding? Honestly,

29
00:01:50,141 --> 00:01:52,977
when I first learned this as a software engineer,

30
00:01:52,977 --> 00:01:56,778
I was a little disappointed. That's it? Autocomplete?

31
00:01:56,778 --> 00:01:59,904
Then I thought about it more and... no, it's actually wild.

32
00:02:01,446 --> 00:02:04,901
Here's the thing. To guess the next word really, really well,

33
00:02:04,901 --> 00:02:06,901
across every topic on the internet,

34
00:02:06,901 --> 00:02:08,992
you have to pick up a lot along the way.

35
00:02:08,992 --> 00:02:12,141
Think about finishing this sentence. "She dropped the glass,

36
00:02:12,141 --> 00:02:15,486
and it..." You need to know glasses break.

37
00:02:15,486 --> 00:02:19,514
"The recipe needs three eggs, but I only have two, so I..."

38
00:02:19,514 --> 00:02:21,956
Now you need a bit of reasoning. To predict well,

39
00:02:21,956 --> 00:02:25,673
the model had to soak up grammar, facts, patterns of logic,

40
00:02:25,673 --> 00:02:28,140
even how people explain things to each other.

41
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:31,170
Nobody programmed those rules in. They got absorbed,

42
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:32,812
because they help with the guessing.

43
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:39,416
There's one more step. After all that reading,

44
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:43,035
the model gets trained again to be a good helper.

45
00:02:43,035 --> 00:02:47,887
People show it examples of great answers, rate its responses,

46
00:02:47,887 --> 00:02:52,595
and it learns to be helpful instead of just continuing random internet text.

47
00:02:52,595 --> 00:02:55,219
That's the difference between a raw text predictor and the polite assistant you chat with.

48
00:02:56,771 --> 00:03:00,852
But here's the part to remember. Even after all that,

49
00:03:00,852 --> 00:03:03,585
it's still predicting what sounds right.

50
00:03:03,585 --> 00:03:08,007
Not looking it up in some giant database of facts. Most of the time,

51
00:03:08,007 --> 00:03:11,595
what sounds right and what is right are the same thing.

52
00:03:11,595 --> 00:03:16,047
The capital of France really is Paris. But sometimes they're not.

53
00:03:16,047 --> 00:03:17,853
And when they're not,

54
00:03:17,853 --> 00:03:22,012
it'll give you the wrong answer in the exact same confident voice as the right one.

55
00:03:23,675 --> 00:03:27,793
Which is exactly what the next video is about. Why AI makes things up,

56
00:03:27,793 --> 00:03:29,858
why it sounds so sure when it does,

57
00:03:29,858 --> 00:03:32,805
and how you can catch it every time. I gotchu.

